Pages = {18-39},
Month = {FEB},
Abstract = {How do identities affect our political perceptions? Drawing on the
   social identity theory, we offer a new notion and empirical modeling
   strategy to study divergence in party placement perceptions. Our
   framework builds on the idea that membership in social groups has the
   potential to act as a social identity by systematically structuring
   perceptual divergence among groups. We conceptualize two consequences of
   social identity formation, ingroup favoritism and outgroup hostility: an
   ingroup effect brings a party closer to the preferred policy of the
   group; an outgroup effect pushes the party away. The approach permits
   detecting these effects and pinpoints the group-based characteristics
   producing perceptual polarization in multiparty systems using standard
   public opinion survey data. An application to the Basque region of Spain
   shows that over two decades, social identities around national
   sentiments and religion have produced the most perceptual divergence and
   polarization, whereas gender or social class do not structure party
   perceptions in the region.},
